    title: "Negotiating a Deal",
    level: "Intermediate",
    category: "Work",
    readTime: 8,
    speakTime: 6,
    story: [
      "David had reviewed the supplier contract so many times that the numbers had started to blur together on the page. As the new procurement manager at Fairview Manufacturing, this was his first real negotiation, and the stakes felt uncomfortably high: a twelve percent price increase from their main steel supplier, one they'd worked with for nearly a decade, was on the table, and David's boss had made it clear that accepting it without at least attempting to push back wasn't really an option.",
      "The call was scheduled for two in the afternoon with Robert Chen, the supplier's regional sales director, a man David had spoken with exactly twice before, both times briefly and pleasantly. This conversation, he suspected, would be neither brief nor entirely pleasant.",
      "\"David, good to hear from you,\" Robert said warmly when the call connected. \"I understand you wanted to discuss the new pricing.\" \"I do,\" David said, keeping his voice steady despite the slight tremor in his hands. \"We've valued this partnership for almost ten years, and I want to keep working together long-term. But a twelve percent increase is significant, and I need to understand what's driving it before we can agree to move forward.\"",
      "Robert explained, at some length, about rising raw material costs and shipping delays affecting the entire industry. David listened carefully, taking notes, resisting the urge to interrupt or immediately counter. When Robert finished, David responded calmly. \"That's helpful context, and I understand the industry pressures. Here's my concern, though — a twelve percent increase would put real strain on our margins this year, and I'd like to explore whether there's room to find a number that works better for both of us.\"",
      "There was a brief pause on the line. \"What number did you have in mind?\" Robert asked. David had prepared for this exact question the night before, rehearsing it until it felt natural rather than scripted. \"Based on typical industry increases we've seen reported this year, something closer to six or seven percent would feel fair, especially given our order volume and the length of our relationship.\"",
      "\"Six or seven is a significant gap from twelve,\" Robert said, though his tone remained professional rather than defensive. \"Let me be honest with you, David — I can't get approval for anything below nine on my end, not with current material costs.\" David considered this, resisting the instinct to immediately agree just to end the tension. \"What if we met at eight, with a two-year contract instead of one? That gives you pricing stability to plan around, and it gives us a number we can actually work with.\"",
      "Another pause, longer this time. \"A two-year commitment does help our planning,\" Robert admitted. \"Let me run the numbers on our end and see what I can do. I'll be honest, eight is close to something I could probably get approved, especially with the longer term attached.\" \"I appreciate you working with me on this,\" David said, feeling something loosen slightly in his chest for the first time since the call began. \"I think a two-year deal at eight percent could genuinely work well for both companies.\"",
      "They spent another fifteen minutes discussing details — payment terms, delivery schedules, a review clause in case material costs shifted dramatically again. By the end of the call, they'd agreed in principle to an eight percent increase with a two-year term, pending final approval from both sides, along with a built-in review after twelve months. \"I'll get the revised contract over to you by Thursday,\" Robert said. \"Pleasure doing business with someone who does their homework, David.\"",
      "After hanging up, David sat back in his chair, letting out a long breath he felt like he'd been holding for the entire forty-minute call. He hadn't gotten everything he'd initially hoped for — a part of him had wanted to push harder toward six percent — but he'd moved the number down by a third, secured better contract terms, and kept the relationship genuinely positive rather than adversarial. His boss, stopping by his desk an hour later after hearing the summary, simply said, \"Good work. That's a solid outcome for your first one.\"",
      "Walking home that evening, David thought about how differently he'd imagined negotiation before this — as something combative, a battle to be won outright. What he'd actually experienced felt more like a careful, honest conversation, two people with different pressures trying to find a number that let both of them keep working together well into the future. He hadn't needed aggressive tactics or clever tricks. He'd needed preparation, patience, and the simple willingness to ask a direct, respectful question: what if we found something that works better for both of us?"
    ],
