      "Ravi had known about the company town hall for three weeks, which should have been plenty of time to prepare, and yet somehow the panic still arrived fresh and overwhelming the night before, as if he were hearing the news for the first time. As the newest member of the product team, he'd been asked to present the results of a six-month project to the entire company — nearly two hundred people, some in the room, most watching remotely through a screen that felt, somehow, even more intimidating than a live audience.",
      "He'd built the slides weeks ago, refined them twice, and knew the content better than almost anyone in the building. The problem wasn't the material. The problem was the version of himself that seemed to appear the moment he imagined standing at the front of that room — hands shaking, voice cracking, mind going completely, humiliatingly blank in front of colleagues whose respect he badly wanted to keep.",
      "That evening, unable to focus on anything else, he called his mentor from a previous job, a woman named Catherine who had once given a presentation so calm and engaging that Ravi still remembered details from it years later. \"I don't know how you make it look so easy,\" he admitted. \"I've practiced my presentation ten times, and I still feel like I'm going to freeze completely.\"",
      "Catherine laughed gently. \"I still get nervous before every single presentation, even now. The nerves don't fully go away — you just learn what to do with them.\" \"What do you mean?\" Ravi asked. \"Practicing content is important, but it's not actually the main thing that helps,\" she explained. \"What helps is practicing the beginning specifically, out loud, until it's almost automatic. If you can get through the first thirty seconds smoothly, your body usually calms down enough to carry the rest.\"",
      "That night, Ravi practiced his opening line perhaps fifteen times, standing in his living room, phone recording him for playback he mostly found painful to watch. \"Good morning, everyone. My name is Ravi, and over the next fifteen minutes, I'm going to walk you through what our team learned building the new onboarding flow — including a few surprises we didn't expect.\" By the tenth repetition, the words had stopped feeling like something he was reading and started feeling like something he actually meant.",
      "The next morning arrived faster than he wanted. Standing backstage, watching the previous presenter finish, Ravi felt his heart pounding uncomfortably in his chest, his palms slightly damp against his laptop. He thought about Catherine's advice, took three slow breaths the way she'd suggested, and walked out when his name was called.",
      "The lights felt brighter than he'd imagined, the sea of faces both smaller and larger than he'd pictured. \"Good morning, everyone,\" he began, and his voice, to his genuine surprise, came out steadier than expected. \"My name is Ravi, and over the next fifteen minutes...\" The rehearsed opening carried him smoothly into his first slide, and somewhere around the second minute, something shifted — the panic hadn't disappeared entirely, but it had quieted into something more manageable, almost like background noise instead of the main event.",
      "Halfway through, someone in the audience asked an unexpected question about implementation timelines, something not covered in his slides. For a brief second, the old fear flickered — what if he didn't know the answer, what if this was the moment everything fell apart. Instead, he paused, genuinely considered the question, and answered honestly. \"That's a great question. We're still finalizing the exact timeline with engineering, but I can follow up with specifics after this session.\" The questioner nodded, satisfied, and Ravi continued, unbroken.",
      "By the time he reached his final slide — a summary of results and next steps — fifteen minutes had somehow passed both slower and faster than he'd expected. \"Thank you for your time,\" he finished, and the room offered a round of applause that felt, to his slightly disbelieving ears, genuinely warm rather than merely polite. His manager caught him afterward in the hallway. \"That was excellent, Ravi. Confident, clear, and you handled that question really well.\"",
      "Walking back to his desk, laptop under his arm, Ravi thought about the version of himself from the night before, convinced he would freeze completely in front of two hundred people. That version hadn't been entirely wrong — the fear had been real, the shaking hands genuine. But Catherine had been right too: the nerves hadn't disappeared. He'd simply learned, through fifteen repetitions of a single opening line, exactly what to do with them."
